# Line endings are LF in the working tree on every platform, not just in the object store.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Several tests are source watchers: they read a file in this repository
# and assert that the code still says what a comment, a README or a runbook claims it says.
# One of them looks for the literal "\n}\n" to find where a guard function ends. With Git's
# default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, a checkout rewrites the working tree to CRLF, that
# search stops matching, and the watcher fails on a repository whose code is perfectly fine —
# a red build that says nothing about the software. It happened here, on a plain
# stash/pop round trip.
#
# The alternative was to make sixteen watchers each normalise line endings before searching.
# That is the same fix written sixteen times, and the seventeenth watcher would forget. One
# declaration removes the entire class instead: everyone who clones this repository gets the
# same bytes the tests were written against.
#
# Binary assets are listed explicitly so this normalisation never touches them.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.mp4 binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.db binary
*.sqlite binary
